<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- FSharp.Analyzers.SDK 0.43 supports net10.0 only -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!-- NuGet convention for F# analyzers: FSAC/CLI load them from
         analyzers/dotnet/fs. IncludeBuildOutput=false + the explicit None
         packs below keep the dlls FLAT in that folder (BuildOutputTargetFolder
         alone nests them under net10.0/, one directory below the path every
         setup guide tells users to configure). -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
    <PackageId>FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers</PackageId>
    <Description>Functional refactoring suggestions (light bulb quick fixes) for F#, built on FSharp.Analyzers.SDK. Over 180 rules covering correctness, performance, idiom and redundancy. Suggestions are Hint severity and never gate a build; the priority rules show as warnings in the editor.</Description>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>0.8.58: Fixes for the shapes the dotnet/fsharp and dotnet/aspnetcore code bases broke: FR0139 reads a piped fold&apos;s collection, not its state; FR0140 leaves a one-line let ... in; FR0007 sees &amp;x on a primed name, and FR0009, FR0030, FR0004, FR0181 and FR0119 a primed name their check asks about; FR0164, FR0119, FR0070, FR0016 and FR0142 stand down on an exit after more statements, a sync call beside its awaited twin, a memory-backed writer, a type handed to reflection and a task that is not FSharp.Core&apos;s where the test stands. FR0133, FR0142, FR0147, FR0157, FR0169, FR0092 and FR0058 now scale linearly with the file.

 0.8.57: Statement chains are read whole: FR0119 rewrites a statement after another statement or a do!, and FR0118, FR0049, FR0163, FR0055, FR0164 and FR0099 see every statement. FR0119 never binds a twin the call sits in, a same-file fire-and-forget wrapper, the sync branch of a dual sync/async body, a call commented as sync on purpose, or Microsoft.Data.Sqlite. FR0049 leaves the sync branch of a dual body and a WhenAny-checked task. FR0118&apos;s loop note leaves an explicit CancellationToken.None, follows carriers by type, and the None fix leaves a compensation before a raise. FR0092 drops the closing mark before &quot;, calling&quot;. FR0162 leaves a mutable whose address is taken. FR0044 and FR0009 respect the builder and FSharp.Core version.

 0.8.56: One build, for FSharp.Analyzers.SDK 0.43 (FsAutoComplete 0.87+, fsharp-analyzers 0.43); 0.8.55 is the last release for SDK 0.41. FR0118 leaves Stream&apos;s own calls (WriteAsync, CopyToAsync) on a stream over memory: a MemoryStream, or an immutable binding made from memory streams only.

 0.8.55: FR0119 leaves in-memory streams and writers, the console, EF Core Add/AddRange and members named for their synchronous work; FR0119 and FR0049 bind with .ConfigureAwait(false) where the task body&apos;s own binds all do. FR0075 leaves an object of the solution&apos;s own type built with a null argument, and trusts a System.* callee only from an assembly signed with a runtime key. FR0160 stays a note where the try sets an HTTP header. FR0007 keeps mutable on a local an inactive #if branch writes. FR0118 leaves the explicit CancellationToken.None of a call named for cleanup. FR0124 lets Serilog read a repeated {0} once in an all-positional template, and counts every hole for Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.

 0.8.54: The fsharp-refactor tool gains --exclude-codes; the analyzers are unchanged.

 0.8.53: A generator&apos;s banner is read at the end of a header line (`module X // generated by ...`) and below an attribute of several lines.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <Authors>Tuomas Hietanen</Authors>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Thorium/fsharp-refactor</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Thorium/fsharp-refactor</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageTags>fsharp;analyzers;refactoring;codefix;static-analysis;code-quality;linter;ionide;fsautocomplete</PackageTags>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Unlicense</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <!-- analyzers are build-time tools: nothing flows to the consumer's output.
         The host supplies FSharp.Analyzers.SDK, FSharp.Compiler.Service and
         FSharp.Core, so the package declares no dependency: a declared one
         lands in the consumer's bin and raises its FSharp.Core floor. -->
    <DevelopmentDependency>true</DevelopmentDependency>
    <SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>true</SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>
    <!-- analyzer packages intentionally have no lib/ folder -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5128</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <!-- One build, for FSharp.Analyzers.SDK 0.43: FsAutoComplete 0.87+
         and fsharp-analyzers 0.43. The SDK loads only analyzers
         built against its own major.minor. -->
    <None
      Include="bin\$(Configuration)\net10.0\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.dll"
      Pack="true"
      PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/fs"
      Visible="false" />
    <None
      Include="bin\$(Configuration)\net10.0\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.xml"
      Pack="true"
      PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/fs"
      Visible="false" />
    <!-- The symbol package: the pdb is packed beside its dll, and NuGet
         copies every packed pdb into the .snupkg at the same path, where the
         symbol server matches it. Without one the .snupkg has neither
         content nor dependencies and the pack fails (NU5017). -->
    <None
      Include="bin\$(Configuration)\net10.0\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.pdb"
      Pack="true"
      PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/fs"
      Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

